# React Native Navigation — Expo Router

## Router model
- Expo Router is required for Expo apps.
- Route files live under `app/`; every route file stays thin and composes named components from `src/features` or `src/components`.
- `_layout.tsx` owns stack/tab/drawer structure and providers at the narrowest useful level.
- Enable typed routes and use absolute hrefs for typed links.

## Exports
- Expo Router route files may use `export default`.
- Reusable components, hooks, services, and feature modules use named exports only.

## Params and links
- Read params with `useLocalSearchParams`; validate and normalize with zod before passing down.
- Prefer `<Link href={...}>` for declarative navigation and `router.push/replace` for event-driven navigation.
- Route params contain ids and filters only — never pass full server entities through navigation.
- Deep links and native intents are parsed at the route boundary and validated before use.

## Screen composition
- Screens own safe-area boundaries, status bar behavior, loading/error/empty states, and navigation options.
- Business logic lives in feature hooks/services, not route files.
- Auth gating happens in layout/session guards and still requires server-side authorization.

## Testing
- Route tests render screens with a router-aware test wrapper.
- E2E flows cover tab changes, stack push/pop, deep-link entry, and auth redirects.
